          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.743,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": "? When will two even numbers add up to give a multiple of 4?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.111,
            0.849,
            0.151
          ],
          "content": "This problem is similar to the question of identifying when adding two numbers will result in an even number. Can you see this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.157,
            0.485,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "There are three cases to examine:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.192,
            0.918,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "<table><tr><td colspan=\"4\">Explanation with Algebra and Visualisation</td><td>Examples 4, 12, 16, 24,</td></tr><tr><td>Adding two (even) numbers that are multiples of 4 will always give a multiple of 4.</td><td>4p and 4q.  $4 p + 4 q$   $= 4 \\: ( p + q ) .$ </td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_1.jpg\"/> <img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_2.jpg\"/></td><td></td><td>36.  $\\begin{array}{l}12 + 16 \\\\= 4\\left(3 + 4\\right) \\\\= 28.\\end{array}$   $\\begin{array}{l}\\frac{46 + 28}{= 4\\ (4 + 7)} \\\\= 44.\\end{array}$ </td></tr><tr><td>Adding two even numbers that are not multiples of 4 will always give a multiple of 4 because their remainders of 2 add up to 4.</td><td> $( 4 p + 2 ) \\; \\mathrm { a n d } \\; ( 4 q + 2 ) .$   $\\begin{array} { r l } & { ( 4 p + 2 ) + ( 4 q + 2 ) } \\\\ & { = 4 p + 4 q + 4 } \\\\ & { = 4   ( p + q + 1 ) . } \\end{array}$ </td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_3.jpg\"/></td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_4.jpg\"/></td><td>2, 6, 10, 18, 22,42.  $2 + 6 = 8.$   $\\begin{array} { l } 6 + 1 0 = 1 6 . \\\\ 2 2 + 6 = 2 8 . \\end{array}$ </td></tr></table>",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_table_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.726,
            0.849,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "What happens when we add a multiple of 4 to an even number that is not a multiple of 4? Is it similar to the case of the parity of the sum of an even and an odd number?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.793,
            0.848,
            0.835
          ],
